{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB04815 - MONDO_1461",
  "verbalization": "Clioquinol is implicated in the treatment of Tinea Corporis through its association with dermatophytosis of the groin and perianal area, which in turn is linked to Tinea Corporis. This relationship is supported by the shared indication between Clioquinol and three antifungal agents—Naftifine, Oxiconazole, and Luliconazole—each of which is indicated for Tinea Corporis. The final scores for these paths suggest a plausible mechanistic link, though the exact mode of action remains unspecified. The presence of a common indication for dermatophytosis, coupled with the shared target of antifungal therapy, implies that Clioquinol may exert antifungal effects through a mechanism that is relevant to Tinea Corporis. The integration of these paths suggests that Clioquinol could potentially be repurposed for the treatment of Tinea Corporis, provided further mechanistic validation is conducted.",
